{"name":"Saffron Mango","hex":"#f9bf58","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"saffron","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant of species Crocus sativus, a crocus.","A spice (seasoning) and colouring agent made from the stigma and part of the style of the plant, sometimes or formerly also used as a dye and insect repellent."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having an orange-yellow colour.","Associated with Hinduism, Hindus or Hindu nationalism."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/saffron"},{"term":"mango","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of species Mangifera indica of tropical Asian fruit trees.","Fruit of the mango tree."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To stuff and pickle (a fruit)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mango"}],"entries":[{"title":"Saffron","blocks":[{"text":"Saffron is a spice derived from the sterile autumn-blooming flower of Crocus sativus, commonly known as the \"saffron crocus\". The vivid crimson stigma and styles are collected and dried for use mainly as a food seasoning and colourant. Saffron crocus was slowly propagated throughout much of Eurasia and later brought to parts of North Africa, North America, even as far as Fiji in Oceania by its Indian migrant descendant population."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Saffron_-_premium_spice.jpg/330px-Saffron_-_premium_spice.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saffron"},{"title":"Mango","blocks":[{"text":"A mango is an edible stone fruit produced by the tropical tree Mangifera indica. It originated in the northeastern part of the South Asia, in what is now Bangladesh, northeastern India and Myanmar. M. indica has been cultivated in South and Southeast Asia since ancient times, resulting in two modern mango cultivar lineages: the \"Indian\" and the \"Southeast Asian\" types. Other species in the genus Mangifera also produce edible fruits called \"mangoes\", most of which are found in the Malesian ecoregion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Mangos_-_single_and_halved.jpg/330px-Mangos_-_single_and_halved.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mango"},{"title":"Saffron (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Saffron is a shade of yellow or orange, the colour of the tip of the saffron crocus thread, from which the spice saffron is derived. The hue of the spice saffron is primarily due to the carotenoid chemical crocin."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saffron_(color)"},{"title":"Shades of yellow","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color yellow may differ in hue, chroma or lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a yellow or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Color_icon_yellow.svg/330px-Color_icon_yellow.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_yellow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Saffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg/500px-Saffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Saffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg/1280px-Saffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Saffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg/500px-Saffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Corms","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASaffron_onions-IMG_7549-2.jpg"},{"srcset":"http